#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "b798cfbb-89ac-5d31-adb5-5c43bd483226" :question/math-academy-id "e-5374" :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "140fc720-22c0-5832-afae-c255144291d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5375" :question/problem "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "4ffaeeac-95a0-598a-9880-166d03f810ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-6779" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "8bff424d-8f2e-528b-acf0-12c33958a132" :question/math-academy-id "e-6817" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9c61364-89e4-5ec3-a514-1fa886fddcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=225\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=225\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285397} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca2e245-1032-5752-ade8-a7803af1ea17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285398} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6b8347-427c-5ff8-8fe1-dfdc0044329b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285399} {:answer/id #uuid "f73e5d31-eedb-523d-9bb1-d5f27f8eea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285400} {:answer/id #uuid "e29315a3-c81d-5a6c-9918-c51edc8afbcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f73e5d31-eedb-523d-9bb1-d5f27f8eea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285400} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c35d5ed-8908-579f-bd21-599749407541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "384a4738-eb41-5445-8951-5cd80374c2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-105915" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $15$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=15.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f46f62bd-7604-53be-a3b1-ec3eba683e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303373} {:answer/id #uuid "41be26dd-81a7-5087-8f03-f8029c54ad61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303374} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3a27ec-134f-5bee-9e09-f888212bc57f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303375} {:answer/id #uuid "1113f6c1-5d17-593e-8040-29d140e56e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303376} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c4b4d4-1519-5898-a716-26de8e522c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4c4b4d4-1519-5898-a716-26de8e522c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303377} :answer-field/id #uuid "776b7cde-eb3a-56a1-a1ee-2446c783da96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e330d13-b7fa-5110-8573-56d34299aa44" :question/math-academy-id "q-106040" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/c36411be4d4fd094d93641a71721a22d1bbc040e15deeefff158915cc68ca52a.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $5$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bcb00fd-f159-5bf4-8043-906d1c3abcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299479} {:answer/id #uuid "50881a7c-5ae8-555d-ba04-7ed120fe5f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299480} {:answer/id #uuid "18f7dc68-6f40-56b8-b9d5-4c3f01828e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299481} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9a090d-aaf9-5394-8e9c-0c7633220598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299482} {:answer/id #uuid "df72ae08-5e83-5454-8bd9-640c7c40ef33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f9a090d-aaf9-5394-8e9c-0c7633220598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299482} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac316a2d-1ff1-5d3c-aeb2-22bd7ef57252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c45d1161-cc6e-56e5-89da-543c3e758d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-106051" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26bd1293-8da9-5c2d-b283-aae2bfe221a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285411} {:answer/id #uuid "1404a4f8-234c-5235-9e86-442511cbcc32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285412} {:answer/id #uuid "48aff435-6b00-5e5c-93cb-877d30e224be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285413} {:answer/id #uuid "2282ca6a-2d5f-5dfa-bc02-69db07b3eff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285414} {:answer/id #uuid "56513223-11da-5186-bb4a-26bc1ad5f3b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26bd1293-8da9-5c2d-b283-aae2bfe221a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285411} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c19efc8-0644-5e8a-abcc-cd4e95d13467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7c4811d-0136-5556-a7aa-255ad6fca4d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-106291" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/3c07f3e732084327f02849cee9355a46ce7a1c5f905d509b1e1404915612c85c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,-2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9178563-1530-5722-836e-3e683572f035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303380} {:answer/id #uuid "31ba1573-e897-5353-8079-4fda64780606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303381} {:answer/id #uuid "682085f3-e6c6-5a9c-a986-a30dd806d7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303382} {:answer/id #uuid "be30d7bd-84ea-5bd7-b974-4492a9eed2f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303383} {:answer/id #uuid "47641c23-fe83-5c2e-9bce-80c458e600a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9178563-1530-5722-836e-3e683572f035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303380} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a8792f3-f6ae-5a7e-9d5a-96bb37db3af4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ab0fea2-8c6c-59ec-a88b-21257097f620" :question/math-academy-id "q-106299" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(y+2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x+1)}^{2}+4{(y+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation of the circle is ${(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "204541c0-24f8-58f7-85be-e4918313b591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285432} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd7c91a-9e6f-5864-9f46-854f138238f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285433} {:answer/id #uuid "b94103fa-ab6c-57be-bbb3-9db306bb275d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285434} {:answer/id #uuid "f796e756-fbad-5213-92fe-34e7bc9736b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285435} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0c64de-4799-5075-aeb1-e9e4bedcd99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "204541c0-24f8-58f7-85be-e4918313b591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285432} :answer-field/id #uuid "11b1e003-da9b-596c-ba40-c91af3644f87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d07f0ee1-8739-5256-bf30-c82b7eeddac5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106300" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbb13dd8-eff9-52f6-969a-6409e4f8cfdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303387} {:answer/id #uuid "75308438-87c3-5cb2-9bc7-86063f55b7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303388} {:answer/id #uuid "45deac2f-9ae7-5d09-86ad-fd452ade04db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2.5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303389} {:answer/id #uuid "75263724-f2b8-5247-99b7-94bcd72f8dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303390} {:answer/id #uuid "58da1099-75d7-5fa0-8647-6636293f3d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75263724-f2b8-5247-99b7-94bcd72f8dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303390} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee44b01-6cd4-5df0-a7a4-def647797914" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12c61f36-cadf-5c27-8401-bbee517fdc13" :question/math-academy-id "q-15802" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $5$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123729e9-f5d9-5267-a64f-8a2c739c1fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "123729e9-f5d9-5267-a64f-8a2c739c1fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303394} :answer-field/id #uuid "60018cf2-e07f-5529-bae6-093443b27ff0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303393} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c1b636-95a7-5665-9890-0894f4d84ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c1b636-95a7-5665-9890-0894f4d84ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303396} :answer-field/id #uuid "30d59160-0947-5b77-bfa2-27a0458b0379" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7914b9ee-25d9-568d-8f8e-0a493023d9f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-275161" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/05a4ea8872b0e1197a248f412b4d1e51a170cc04b2da24aa7f6d05679985a74e.png)\n\nThe arc of the circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/3f259a44786f9851588e212396f1c9fe3259a63c88b8c3090ebc6f1e50a6c456.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d62cf37c-d928-5471-a730-29da52a950e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299500} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fc7a00-dad9-5bc1-8443-11e841f007c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d62cf37c-d928-5471-a730-29da52a950e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299500} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e501c9-f1e9-510e-8202-362c8e8398e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299499} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4412556e-6fb8-579f-ae78-4929da26e1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4412556e-6fb8-579f-ae78-4929da26e1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299502} :answer-field/id #uuid "54e65f65-c0b2-5b94-8cf9-7800c70d0e79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1d3adf2-769c-543a-8d79-ad858b0c058d" :question/math-academy-id "q-321891" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/da3a33a3ade10663064516bac22c07459ccf7ce556753ba94d70ee832e947458.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $10$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(10,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=10.$ Substituting $r=10$ gives\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd457ab8-0181-5b5e-ad2f-f482e00b683b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(\\theta)+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303400} {:answer/id #uuid "659e564d-1f0e-55ea-a92d-07a5da294ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(\\theta)-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "659e564d-1f0e-55ea-a92d-07a5da294ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cos(\\theta)-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303401} :answer-field/id #uuid "e719faf4-002d-516d-a797-2de8c8a4a279" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b706664-71e1-5342-a88b-1e6072f8a3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin(\\theta)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b706664-71e1-5342-a88b-1e6072f8a3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sin(\\theta)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303403} :answer-field/id #uuid "c021c6cd-24a7-5256-b792-7421352b6137" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67c0d567-0a98-5b60-aa47-f735cc300b15" :question/math-academy-id "q-321942" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/59cef258a1ad0e6a0f56c37e0b7020e5341c329d5655c41f49e6e78eafca8b3f.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $6$ centered at $(-5,-2)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(1,-2).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $6$ centered at the point $(-5,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d84cb2b-0a25-5fc4-960e-170a3e2e4aa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d84cb2b-0a25-5fc4-960e-170a3e2e4aa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d33d357-d449-5e18-b3a4-7a9139491d24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fef87a06-3a66-5dff-b54f-a5aecf6b338a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fef87a06-3a66-5dff-b54f-a5aecf6b338a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303408} :answer-field/id #uuid "99de5aba-8f34-55b2-8133-83921c54ab79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303407} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f71130-8e9e-545f-863d-32655c304885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65f71130-8e9e-545f-863d-32655c304885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303410} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d8da69-4abb-55e7-be09-b65c4129c777" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ca6697a-5172-5e08-af3d-aff0cbcd8ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-321988" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(x+3) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(y-1) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37511343-3a89-56ab-b839-1525b4ea9ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37511343-3a89-56ab-b839-1525b4ea9ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303413} :answer-field/id #uuid "9afcc869-c49f-52fb-8874-10c9e350a7dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303412} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c47043-53d4-56e2-a8ab-5b6f50a2619d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c47043-53d4-56e2-a8ab-5b6f50a2619d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303415} :answer-field/id #uuid "883b7e66-b945-5e6a-a4a9-dc30f84c3ec4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303414} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42d8e596-22bc-5a90-a3ab-fc5d023f9856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42d8e596-22bc-5a90-a3ab-fc5d023f9856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303417} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b67d8a5-8504-5dfd-b964-be5b026dfbd0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "381c6a8c-2101-5081-94d4-70ba5b197dd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-321991" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +13,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-13 & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-13)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & =1 \\\\ {(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2}=49.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c72d4b7-bdb7-501b-a53f-79c953e7a862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285485} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c598f0-799f-5e78-bfd1-9da8d427099d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285486} {:answer/id #uuid "3c4b8896-8bd5-59d3-af12-5aec03c58d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285487} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa47a75-ff15-5d00-a43c-7ac86604a667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285488} {:answer/id #uuid "df31011a-9b55-5709-abad-15147cc41dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c72d4b7-bdb7-501b-a53f-79c953e7a862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285485} :answer-field/id #uuid "80db3e06-84ca-50cd-8c9d-7089ac04f59a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d41583ea-4992-5cc5-818e-760f3773a067" :question/math-academy-id "q-6878" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/de2b66f976dc204566516a1724fa19cc03bf8b8f4da1fa4f4a198fd656ad10ca.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(9,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=9.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88204264-a234-5d9d-bbdc-e8465fc6b680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} - y^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086228} {:answer/id #uuid "94212a6e-ccf9-5b85-9c8f-881bb287dc81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086229} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d42504-b259-5f2d-8efb-7c37991fd48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086230} {:answer/id #uuid "fcce5263-2226-53b2-b008-e2570586bc64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086231} {:answer/id #uuid "33cc927b-c1a7-508d-b2a8-5f00058c2158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} - y^{2} = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcce5263-2226-53b2-b008-e2570586bc64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086231} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5f5171e-53ca-5df4-a5b8-a434df329574" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e86c521-0742-549b-8e0a-9a85599dcb9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6879" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =9(1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9860a59-ff3a-5fb2-a3c4-8cf6bc3c1154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3\\cos \\theta  + 1, y = 2\\sin \\theta  + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086719} {:answer/id #uuid "efdff3d9-45f7-5cdc-8a72-6d6bb5697f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\cos \\theta  + 2, y = \\sin \\theta  + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086720} {:answer/id #uuid "17875a63-4c0a-5f67-aee7-13f9159f53ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\cos \\theta  + 3, y = \\sin \\theta  + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086721} {:answer/id #uuid "1e65851a-49dc-5614-bb84-df254c8d2cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\cos \\theta  - 2, y = \\sin \\theta  - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086722} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd82213-58b7-5002-a5f3-75d25e81b16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\cos \\theta  - 3, y = \\sin \\theta  - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17875a63-4c0a-5f67-aee7-13f9159f53ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\cos \\theta  + 3, y = \\sin \\theta  + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086721} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae2e5a73-d0b6-5d96-b83d-2c7078c8660e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbe63a7d-d4cb-5598-8edc-e441310edc62" :question/math-academy-id "q-78204" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1032554-8f94-5d12-8f11-02c1d823ad8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303420} {:answer/id #uuid "d37eb454-ef6f-5b2f-b1bb-1c43aa6c595d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303421} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf4eae6-8ef3-5e82-94e9-119f8f45d5e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303422} {:answer/id #uuid "d57bada9-4757-59fd-a376-7aaee70f75d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303423} {:answer/id #uuid "6b872ded-e93c-571c-adc5-2d9a2bc9b37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d57bada9-4757-59fd-a376-7aaee70f75d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303423} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e2b3692-7376-5673-8ec7-be121125b219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c32b602-47c9-5b39-83b7-5221df83596f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79165" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/c9e8fb87758eefb1facfea08112c5add0cc54d481e36e740d3cc623f05fa41fb.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/8a0491010e71d24ebac54b5b8d03b1ce45f7de90a0f21ff1ea2f0c2babf4ec77.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a75d7f-a83a-53d2-91cf-19783575a28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299545} {:answer/id #uuid "f25996bf-fd27-5e29-89e3-7d488619155e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299546} {:answer/id #uuid "22c3653e-9278-5059-86c9-30d610217067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299547} {:answer/id #uuid "fc478a22-4762-53c5-90b4-bcbae6233313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299548} {:answer/id #uuid "e655fb51-22af-560b-911f-fc4ad0e14612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc478a22-4762-53c5-90b4-bcbae6233313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299548} :answer-field/id #uuid "978b3bf0-a398-5c0c-a0df-b249d600a836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299544}] :questio